WEBVTT

00:03.900 --> 00:07.706
Coding agents can turn language instructions into robot programs,

00:07.706 --> 00:10.575
but they ground their decisions mainly in vision.

00:11.800 --> 00:15.331
Here, a vision-only agent loses its grip on a door handle,

00:15.331 --> 00:19.166
lifts off a vase it is wiping, and loses contact while peeling.

00:19.900 --> 00:30.595
Force and contact measurements complement vision by providing evidence about contact states and mechanical responses that are difficult to infer from visual observations alone.

00:30.845 --> 00:31.901
We present FORCA,

00:31.901 --> 00:38.916
a training-free coding agent that uses wrist force/torque sensing in both program execution and program revision.

00:42.300 --> 00:43.930
It writes a complete task program.

00:44.700 --> 00:48.134
During a rollout, force feedback runs inside the program,

00:48.134 --> 00:49.338
with no model query.

00:49.588 --> 00:50.184
Afterward,

00:50.184 --> 00:53.873
the agent inspects the recorded force and revises the program.

00:57.000 --> 00:59.884
It starts from open-source modules for perception,

00:59.884 --> 01:01.845
grasping, planning, and execution.

01:04.070 --> 01:10.915
We consider three ways in which force/torque feedback can benefit manipulation: improving manipulation accuracy,

01:10.915 --> 01:14.154
adapting to physical changes, and regulating contact.

01:14.404 --> 01:17.657
Our experiments show that FORCA effectively addresses all three.

01:17.950 --> 01:24.424
We use a UR5e with its built-in force/torque sensor and a WSG gripper to conduct experiments.

01:25.200 --> 01:27.774
First, improving manipulation accuracy,

01:27.774 --> 01:36.090
by complementing visual estimates with contact-based geometric information to refine grasp poses and guide subsequent actions.

01:37.200 --> 01:39.758
To open a door, the robot must grasp the handle,

01:39.758 --> 01:41.038
press it down, and pull.

01:42.100 --> 01:47.130
FORCA first touches the door panel to correct its visual estimate of the door position,

01:47.130 --> 01:48.980
and only then grasps the handle.

01:50.700 --> 01:53.006
It then grasps the handle and presses it down.

01:58.200 --> 02:00.323
The vision-only agent presses the handle,

02:00.323 --> 02:02.911
but the bar slips out of its grip during the pull.

02:05.200 --> 02:08.400
FORCA's program, refined from the recorded press force,

02:08.400 --> 02:10.670
opens the door and releases the handle.

02:11.700 --> 02:14.514
Over ten trials, FORCA succeeds every time;

02:14.514 --> 02:16.346
the vision-only agent, once.

02:18.600 --> 02:20.978
Second, adapting to physical changes,

02:20.978 --> 02:27.919
by providing evidence about how object properties affect action outcomes to adjust manipulation accordingly.

02:28.900 --> 02:34.909
A knocking program developed for a cube on a whiteboard is reused when the object or the surface changes.

02:36.500 --> 02:37.474
After each knock,

02:37.474 --> 02:42.629
the agent revises the program from the measured impact force and where the object stopped.

02:44.700 --> 02:47.253
The light cube reaches the target in two knocks;

02:47.253 --> 02:48.955
the heavier cylinder needs four.

02:53.000 --> 02:54.565
Across all four conditions,

02:54.565 --> 02:57.984
the reused program reaches the target within a few retries.

03:01.900 --> 03:03.592
Third, regulating contact,

03:03.592 --> 03:10.879
by using live force measurements to adjust ongoing motion and maintain contact as surfaces vary or objects move.

03:11.800 --> 03:17.578
Wiping programs refined on a static vase are reused on a vase that a person holds and moves.

03:19.500 --> 03:22.462
The vision-only program leaves much of the marker line.

03:22.462 --> 03:23.917
FORCA wipes most of it off.

03:29.500 --> 03:32.650
From the side, the vision-only eraser lifts off the surface,

03:32.650 --> 03:35.066
while FORCA keeps it pressed against the vase.

03:36.800 --> 03:39.852
Contact loss drops from fifty-six percent to under two.

03:40.900 --> 03:42.891
When peeling a hand-held cucumber,

03:42.891 --> 03:47.341
the vision-only blade keeps losing contact, while FORCA follows the surface.

03:51.500 --> 03:53.451
FORCA peels one continuous strip,

03:53.451 --> 03:56.586
and contact loss falls from forty-one percent to two.

03:58.400 --> 04:02.208
Developing each program from scratch took eight to twenty iterations.

04:02.500 --> 04:07.848
Each iteration took four to ten minutes of agent reasoning and one to three minutes of robot motion.

04:14.400 --> 04:15.082
In summary,

04:15.082 --> 04:22.646
FORCA is a training-free coding agent that uses wrist force/torque sensing in both program execution and program revision.

04:23.000 --> 04:27.058
It improves manipulation accuracy, adapts to physical changes,

04:27.058 --> 04:29.546
and regulates contact on a real robot.
